 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Aim: to identify the parts of a flower.\nMaterials: a large flower (hibiscus or flamboyant), a sharp razor blade or scalpel, a hand lens, white paper, glue or tape.\nMethod:\n1. Count and remove the sepals, from the outside.\n2. Remove the petals, one by one.\n3. Remove the stamens and look at an anther with the hand lens; touch it to see the pollen.\n4. Cut the ovary across (or along) with the blade and look at the ovules with the hand lens.\n5. Stick each kind of part on the paper and label it; draw the flower cut in half.\nSafety: cut away from your fingers; wash your hands afterwards.\nA good biological drawing: a title, clear single lines, no shading, label lines that do not cross, and the magnification.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "k": 777
  },
  "problem": "After cutting a flower in half, Ngwa sees small round bodies inside a swollen part at the base of the style. What are they?",
  "steps": [
   "The swollen part at the base of the style is the ovary.",
   "The small round bodies inside it are the ovules, which become seeds after fertilisation."
  ],
  "answer": "Ovules, inside the ovary.",
  "checks": [],
  "figure": "flower(k)"
 },
